  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "1.4.2 - A nested service scope no longer closes its caller's connection scope.\n\nWith per-thread connection scopes (Globals.ConnectionScopePerThread = true), a service scope\nopened and disposed inside a connection scope it did not open - for example\nusing (CEF.NewConnectionScope(new ConnectionScopeSettings { CommandTimeoutOverride = 600 }))\naround code that opens its own using (CEF.NewServiceScope()) - closed that connection scope on\ndisposal and popped it. Every later command in the caller ran on a new standalone scope, so the\ncaller's CommandTimeoutOverride silently stopped applying (the command timeout fell back to\nGlobals.CommandTimeoutSeconds or the connect timeout). A service scope now closes only a\nconnection scope it did not inherit. A connection scope opened inside the service scope and left\nopen is still closed with it. No public API change; AssemblyVersion stays 1.4.0.0.\n\n1.4.1 - Fixes a permanent ServiceScope leak.\n\nServiceScope.Dispose advertised disposal as its LAST statement, but that callback is\nwhat pops the scope off the ambient stack and restores the previous current scope. Any\nthrow before it skipped the pop, so the scope stayed rooted for the life of the async\nflow and the current-scope pointer was left referencing a disposed scope. The unwind now\nruns in a finally.\n\nThe throw that exposed it: disposal asked \"is there a connection scope to close?\" through\nthe create-on-read CEF.CurrentConnectionScope, so a scope that never opened a connection\nwould manufacture one during its own disposal - and that constructor throws when no data\nprovider is configured. Disposal now peeks instead of creating, matching what the other\nbranch already did.\n\nMeasured on an affected workload: ambient scope depth went from +22.00 per call (dead\nlinear) to 0.00, managed memory from 350->645MB and climbing to flat, and calls ran about\n20% faster with the spurious connection scopes gone.\n\nNo public API change; AssemblyVersion intentionally stays 1.4.0.0 so existing binding is\nunaffected. System.Text.Json and System.Collections.Immutable are no longer forced on\nnet8.0 consumers - they are now referenced only for netstandard2.0 and net462, which is\nwhere they are actually needed.",
